WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.816
Idea. A correlation means two things change together. On hot days, more ice cream is sold, and more people get sunburn. They rise together. But ice cream does not cause sunburn. Hot weather causes both. So a correlation does not prove a cause.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:34.742
Good evidence also needs good data. A sample is the group of people tested. A small sample may not be reliable. Other factors, like age, diet or exercise, can change the result. And body readings go up and down, so one reading is not enough.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:44.613
Method. Here is the method. Step one: read the claim. Underline exactly what it says will happen. Every mark is about that claim.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:54.335
Step two: find the data that test the claim. Quote one or two real figures, with their units. Your opinion earns nothing. The numbers do.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:01:16.147
Step three: say what the data cannot show. Name the problem. Is the sample small? Could other factors change the result? Do the values vary? Is a time point missing? If every reading is already past the healthy limit, the data cannot show when the problem began. And if values go up and down, a prediction from the trend is uncertain.

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:25.910
Step four: give a judgement. The data support the claim, partly support it, or do not support it. A discuss question always needs this.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:43.575
Sometimes you must find an anomalous result. An anomalous result is one value that does not fit the pattern of the others. In this made-up example, the values rise steadily, but reading four is far too low. Name the value, and say it does not fit.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:02:04.436
Worked example. Now a real exam question. The table shows the substances in urine over one day, on a high protein diet and on a low protein diet. The claim: muscle breaks down at a steady rate, and diet does not change it. Discuss whether the table supports this. Four marks.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:15.537
Here is the table. Look at each row. Urea is fourteen point seven grams on the high protein diet, but only two point two grams on the low protein diet.

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:24.121
Uric acid and ammonia also change a lot. So these three come mostly from protein in the food. They depend on diet.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:41.061
Now creatinine. It is zero point five eight grams and zero point six zero grams. Only zero point zero two grams apart, on both diets. Creatinine is likely to come from muscle breakdown. So the table supports the claim.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:47.227
Here is the full answer. It is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:56.934
Common mistake. A common mistake: two lines rise together, so you write that one causes the other. A link is not proof of cause.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:05.852
Another mistake is to write only that the results are not reliable. Always say why. For example, too few people were tested.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:25.515
Your turn. A company says its new drink lowers blood glucose. Four people drink it for four weeks. Their mean blood glucose falls from six point zero to five point six. They also started to exercise more. Discuss whether the data support the claim. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:38.271
Answer. First, the evidence. The mean, or average, blood glucose falls by zero point four, from six point zero to five point six. This agrees with the claim.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:50.092
Now the limits. Only four people were tested. And they also exercised more, and exercise lowers blood glucose too. So the data only partly support the claim.

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:57.768
So, every time: read the claim. Quote the data. Name what the data cannot show. Then give your judgement.
